.TH CHTTPSVR_REQ_PEER_CERT_SUBJECT 3 "2026-10-01" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_req_peer_cert_subject \- give the subject of the verified client certificate
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

const char *chttpsvr_req_peer_cert_subject(const chttpsvr_req *req);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Gives the subject of the client certificate of the request as an RFC
2253 distinguished name.  The name lists its attributes from the most
specific to the least, for example

    CN=alice,OU=staff,O=Example

Every byte above 0x7F, every control character and every character
that RFC 2253 reserves is escaped with a backslash, so the string is
printable ASCII, and a name that a client crafted cannot pose as
another one.  An organization "Example, Inc." reads as
O=Example\e, Inc.  Compare the subject as a whole string: a substring
of a subject can come from any of its attributes.

It gives the subject only when chttpsvr_req_peer_cert_verified(3)
reports true for req.  The subject belongs to the connection, so every
request on one keep-alive connection gives the same text, and a
resumed TLS session gives the subject that the handshake which
established it verified.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
req  The request handle.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A NUL-terminated string that stays valid for the lifetime of the
request.  Do not free it.

NULL in every case where chttpsvr_req_peer_cert_verified(3) reports
false: a plaintext connection, a server with no ca_bundle_path, a
client that presented no certificate, and a NULL req.  NULL also when
an allocation fails; call chttpsvr_req_peer_cert_verified(3) to tell
the two apart.
.fi
.SH THREAD SAFETY
.nf
Call it only on the thread that runs the handler or the middleware of
req, as every other accessor of a request.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>
#include <string.h>

static void handler(chttpsvr_req *req, chttpsvr_resp *resp, void *ctx)
{
    (void)ctx;
    const char *who = chttpsvr_req_peer_cert_subject(req);
    if (!who || strcmp(who, "CN=billing,O=Example") != 0) {
        chttpsvr_resp_set_status(resp, CHTTP_STATUS_FORBIDDEN);
        return;
    }
    chttpsvr_resp_write_str(resp, "ok");
}
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_req_peer_cert_verified (3),
.BR chttpsvr_req_peer_cert_der (3),
.BR chttpsvr_req_peer_cert_sha256 (3)
